As I considered how to handle string expressions w/o a string keyword, I rejected the idea of using opMethods like = or += to trigger string concatenation. My reason for the rejection is I do not want to give up shortcuts and I want to keep print and string expressions identical. To do that I figured I would have to use the PrintXP rule. That led me to an interesting grammar change. The new StringXP rule looks like:
        StringXP    ","- stuff=PrintXP+ defer;
The comma replaces the string keyword and it allows rule guards to skip over StringXP; the , is a shortcut that does nothing, and more relevant, it is not an allowable Token. Instead of sharing the print action, I wrote the following StringXP action. It duplicates much of the print action, but no biggie it is short, and no if statement needed to figure out what print method to invoke.
/*******************************************************************************
	Immediate method for the StringXP rule.
        StringXP    ","- stuff=PrintXP+ defer;
*******************************************************************************/
extern GroupItem aCTionStringXP(GroupItem input)
{
GroupRules  ruler   = groupRules;
GroupItem   stuff:;
GroupItem   grup;
Buffer      buffer = bufferSTAK.pop();
    if !buffer      buffer = new("print buffer");
    while grup = stuff.nextAttribute(grup) {
        if noPrint  continue;
        GroupItem   FormaT:,
                    result,
                    ExpressioN:;
        if ExpressioN {
            if isMethod result = method(ExpressioN);
            else        result = ExpressioN;
            appendGroup(result,FormaT,buffer); }
        else            appendGroup(grup,FormaT,buffer); }
    stuff.clear();
    return opString(stuff,buffer);
}

Also I removed the useDefaultSpace = true; statements from opPrint and opString so now $ is a toggle that persists. It means the user has to be aware, and may have to turn it on after turning it off, but that can happen at the end of the print statement or at the beginning of the next print statement; Also, useDefaultSpace = true; is run by processAction before the action runs so it gets set back to default on every action invocation. It pays immediate benefits in spellLeaf that looks like the following after incorporating the changes. Note the second $ that toggles useDefaultSpace true happens on a print statement that only needs the $ to reset the toggle status. The reformatting is only here, and done because I am anal about how I want my code to look; the code the minion produced is still in genEmit.

    spellLeaf argument code={
        :argument at sink slot site;
        local   = string $"t" at;
        kind    = argument.taG;
        if kind == "OPT";
            wrapped := argument.firstMembeR;
            wrapped :% sink;
            piece = this(wrapped);
            leaf = ,"(" piece _ "|| 1)";
        or kind == "MANY";      leaf = ,"many" site "(" sink "," local ")";
        or kind == "CALL";      leaf = ,"parseR(" local "," sink ")";
        or kind == "LIT";       leaf = ,"lit(" local "," '"' argument '"' ")";
        or kind == "LITTO";
            if sink == "into";  leaf = ,"litOption(" local ",into," '"' argument '"' ")";
            else                leaf = ,"litTo(" local ",label," '"' argument '"' "," '"' slot '"' ")";
        else    print $"emitLeaf: no emission for plan kind" kind:;
        return leaf;
        };

